            <tr><td data-label="Job"><strong>New radiator in a new position</strong></td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">From £325</td><td data-label="Notes">New pipework from the circuit</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Job"><strong>TRV fitted</strong></td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">£65 each, or up to 6 for £295</td><td data-label="Notes">Set the heat room by room</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Job"><strong>Radiator repairs and leaks</strong></td><td data-label="Price inc VAT">First hour £120</td><td data-label="Notes">£150 evenings and weekends, £180 bank holidays</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>What is included</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>The new radiator and all fittings (supply and fit jobs)</li>
        <li>Old radiator taken off and taken away</li>
        <li>Floors protected and the area left clean</li>
        <li>Inhibitor checked and topped up</li>
        <li>System refilled, bled and balanced</li>
        <li>A 12-month workmanship guarantee</li>
      </ul>
      <p>There is no call-out fee. A straight swap can often be priced on the phone. For moves, new radiators and column styles, we visit first. The quote visit is free, and the written price is fixed.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="homes">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What radiators suit Wirral homes?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">It depends on the house. A Birkenhead terrace, a Bebington semi and a Heswall detached home each need something different. We work out the heat each room needs before we suggest a radiator.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Bay windows in Birkenhead and Wallasey</h3>
          <p>In CH41 to CH44 terraces, the most common complaint is a cold front room. Often the radiator is on a side wall, or it is too small. A big bay window lets in a lot of cold. A radiator of the right size, under the bay, warms that cold air as it falls. We check the size and the spot at the quote visit.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Period houses in Hoylake and West Kirby</h3>
          <p>Homes in CH47 and CH48 often have high ceilings and original coving. Modern panels can look wrong there. A two or three-column radiator suits the room. Taller columns give more heat for the wall space. We size for heat first, then for looks. Where we can, we run pipes under the timber floor.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Large rooms in Heswall and south Wirral</h3>
          <p>Detached homes in Heswall, Pensby and Bromborough often have big rooms. Extensions are often left with a small radiator. A radiator that is too small will never warm a big room, however hot the boiler runs. We size it right, and check the boiler can cope with the extra load.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Microbore pipes in Prenton, Moreton and Bebington</h3>
          <p>Many 1970s and 1980s estate homes in CH43, CH46 and CH63 have thin 8mm or 10mm pipes. Adding a radiator to these needs the right fittings. We also check the pipes and pump can carry the extra radiator. We do this before we start.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="types">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Which radiator is right for me?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">For most rooms, a double panel radiator gives the most heat for the money. Column and designer radiators are about the look, so check the heat output first.</p>
      <ul class="brx-list">
        <li><strong>Panel radiators.</strong> The most common type on the Wirral. Double panels give lots of heat for their size. This is what our £245 price covers.</li>
        <li><strong>Column radiators.</strong> Two, three and four-column styles, from 600mm up to 1800mm tall. They suit Victorian and Edwardian rooms.</li>
        <li><strong>Designer and vertical radiators.</strong> Good where wall space is tight. Many give less heat than a panel radiator, so we size them with care.</li>
        <li><strong>Towel rails.</strong> In chrome, white or anthracite. A dual-fuel rail can run on its own in summer. It needs a safe electrical supply. Our partner firm <a href="https://www.idealelectricalsolutions.co.uk/">Ideal Electrical Solutions</a> can fit that.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Should I fit TRVs?</h3>
      <p>Yes, in most homes. A TRV sets the heat for each room. Keep the living room warm and the spare room cool. They also stop sunny rooms in Heswall and West Kirby getting too hot in spring. TRVs are £65 each, or up to six for £295. We leave one radiator without a TRV, often in the hall, so water can always flow.</p>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>Buying online?</strong> Compare the heat output in watts or BTU, not the size. Two radiators the same width can give very different heat. Tell us the room and we will check it.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="how">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How does a radiator installation work?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">A straight swap takes 1 to 2 hours. Five radiators are usually done in one day.</p>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Call for a price.</strong> A straight swap can often be priced on the phone. For moves, column radiators or new pipework, we visit and check the pipe route.</li>
        <li><strong>Fixed written price.</strong> It covers the radiator, fittings, labour and balancing. No add-ons on the day.</li>
        <li><strong>Drain and remove (30 minutes).</strong> We drain just that radiator where we can. If the valves will not hold, we drain the system.</li>
        <li><strong>Fit the new radiator (30 to 60 minutes).</strong> New brackets go up and the radiator is connected. Floors are covered.</li>
        <li><strong>Refill and balance (30 minutes).</strong> We top up the inhibitor, refill and bleed. Then we balance every radiator.</li>
        <li><strong>Old radiator taken away.</strong> We tidy up before we leave.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>Need new radiators on the Wirral?</strong> Call for a fixed price, from £245 including VAT.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
      <h3>Do I need a power flush first?</h3>
      <p>If your radiators are cold at the bottom, or bleed black water, yes. Sludge will soon clog new radiators. If they heat well and the water is clear, there is no need. See our <a href="/power-flushing-wirral">power flushing prices on the Wirral</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="repairs">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What are the signs a Wirral radiator needs fixing?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Cold patches, leaks, rust and noise are the main signs. Repairs are charged by time, from £120 for the first hour on weekdays 9am to 4pm. There is no call-out fee.</p>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li><strong>Cold at the bottom.</strong> Sludge has settled. Bleeding will not fix it. A flush will.</li>
        <li><strong>Cold at the top.</strong> Air in the radiator. Bleed it. If it keeps coming back, air is getting in, and that needs a check.</li>
        <li><strong>Stone cold.</strong> Often a stuck TRV pin or a closed valve. Usually a quick fix.</li>
        <li><strong>Leaking valve.</strong> Often a loose gland nut or worn valve. In a house with rooms below, fix it fast.</li>
        <li><strong>Rust or pinholes.</strong> The radiator is rusting from inside. It needs replacing, not repairing.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The first hour is £150 evenings, nights and weekends, and £180 on bank holidays. If a job needs more time, we tell you the price before we carry on.</p>
      <div class="brx-danger"><p><strong>Radiator leaking now?</strong> Put a bowl and towels under it. Turn both valves clockwise to close them. If it will not stop, turn the water off at the stopcock and call <a href="tel:01515580334">{PHONE}</a>.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="wirral">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Where on the Wirral do you fit radiators?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Every CH postcode on the peninsula, from Birkenhead to Neston. The price is the same wherever you live.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table stk">
          <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Postcodes</th><th>Typical homes</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td data-label="Area"><strong>Birkenhead, Rock Ferry, Prenton, Wallasey, New Brighton</strong></td><td data-label="Postcodes">CH41 to CH45</td><td data-label="Typical homes">Victorian and Edwardian terraces, bay windows</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Area"><strong>Moreton, Hoylake, West Kirby</strong></td><td data-label="Postcodes">CH46 to CH48</td><td data-label="Typical homes">Period houses with high ceilings, estate homes</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="Area"><strong>Heswall, Pensby, Bromborough, Bebington, Neston</strong></td><td data-label="Postcodes">CH60 to CH64</td><td data-label="Typical homes">Larger detached homes and extensions</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Our heating engineers are part of our team led by James. All work on the heating circuit is done by our Gas Safe registered engineers. You can check any engineer on the <a href="https://www.gassaferegister.co.uk">Gas Safe Register</a>. We carry £2 million public liability insurance.</p>
      <p>Want a whole new system? See <a href="/central-heating-installation-wirral">central heating installation on the Wirral</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceGuide>
